Guidelines:
- Derive dates from the content; if an exact date is unknown, use the best-supported
  year or relative ordering. Keep events in chronological order.
- Give each date only the precision the sources support: a bare year ("1356") when
  that is all they say. Do not invent a month or a day to make dates look uniform.
- Keep labels short and factual; the second line is what "detail" is for.
- Cover the whole span evenly. If the content supports events across a long period,
  take them from throughout it rather than clustering on the best-documented
  stretch and leaving decades unrepresented — a timeline whose events all fall in
  one short window, with a single distant outlier, tells the reader nothing about
  the period in between. Where a long quiet stretch is genuine, that is fine; where
  the sources do describe events in it, include them.

